---
// Encryption — the crypto reference (Unit 9).
import Base from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const crumbs = `<nav class="crumbs"><a href="../index.html">Docs</a> &gt; <a href="../unit-09/index.html">Unit 9</a> &gt; <span>Encryption</span></nav>`;

const body = `<h1>Encryption</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Your repo crossed the public internet unread by every router
    along the way — because of a padlock icon and one mathematically
    stubborn idea from the 1970s. This is the reference for symmetric
    vs asymmetric keys, the TLS handshake, hashes, and every phrasing
    of the question the exam uses.
</p>

<h2>Symmetric — one shared key</h2>
<p>
    The same key encrypts and decrypts. Fast, simple, and the cipher
    you have already built by hand (Caesar: shift letters by 3). Its
    fatal flaw is not strength — it is <strong>key
    distribution</strong>: to agree on a shared key you already need
    a private channel, and the public internet never offered one.
</p>
<pre><code>message + key → ciphertext  →  ciphertext + key → message

CAT  → (+3) → FDW  →  FDW  → (−3) → CAT</code></pre>
<p>
    Caesar dies not to brute force but to the <strong>statistics of
    language</strong> — frequency analysis. The most common letters
    in English are ETAOIN; match their ciphertext patterns and the
    key falls out. Every real symmetric cipher since scrambles the
    pattern, but the lesson remains: a cipher that preserves any
    structure is a puzzle, not a lock.
</p>

<h2>Asymmetric — the public pair</h2>
<p>
    The breakthrough: generate a KEY PAIR where one key encrypts and
    only the other decrypts. You publish the PUBLIC key (literally —
    on your website, in a certificate); you keep the PRIVATE key
    secret. Anyone can lock a message to you; nobody but you can
    open it. Publishing the lock reveals nothing about the key —
    that asymmetry rests on one math fact: multiplying two huge
    primes is easy, but factoring their product back into those
    primes is intractable for any computer you can buy.
</p>
<table>
    <tr><th></th><th>Symmetric (e.g. AES)</th><th>Asymmetric (e.g. RSA)</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Keys</td><td>One shared secret</td><td>Public + private pair</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Speed</td><td>Very fast</td><td>Slow</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Solves</td><td>Bulk encryption</td><td>Key exchange + identity</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Struggles with</td><td>Getting the key there</td><td>Volume</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>TLS — using both, for the best of both</h2>
<p>
    HTTPS is the two working together. The asymmetric part builds
    trust once per session; the symmetric part does the actual work.
    The handshake, in six lines:
</p>
<pre><code>1. browser → server:          "hello"
2. server → browser:          certificate + public key
3. browser verifies the certificate against a trusted authority
4. browser:                   generates random session key,
                              encrypts it with the server's PUBLIC key
5. server:                    decrypts it with its PRIVATE key
                              (the only being that can)
6. both sides:                switch to fast symmetric, session key only</code></pre>
<p>
    Step 3 is what "certificate" means: a third party vouches that
    this public key really belongs to github.com. Without that
    verification, anyone on your wifi could impersonate the site and
    hand you THEIR key — the man-in-the-middle attack. When your
    browser says a certificate is invalid, step 3 failed, and the
    correct move is to close the tab, not click through.
</p>

<h2>Hashing — encryption's cousin that is NOT encryption</h2>
<p>
    A hash is a one-way fingerprint: any input becomes a fixed-size
    output, and no reasonable path leads back.
    <code>password123</code> → <code>ef92b778bafe...</code>. There
    is no key because there is no decryption — login works by
    hashing what you type and comparing fingerprints. Salting
    (mixing a random per-user value in before hashing) makes every
    stored hash different even for identical passwords, defeating
    the precomputed-table attack. The exam phrasing to know: a hash
    provides <strong>integrity</strong> (did this change?) and
    <strong>password storage</strong>, never secrecy.
</p>

<h2>The exam-question shapes</h2>
<ul>
    <li>"Which is true of public key encryption?" — the public key
        cannot decrypt what it encrypted; only the private key can</li>
    <li>"Why are symmetric and asymmetric EACH used in a secure
        transaction?" — asymmetric to agree on identity and exchange
        a session key; symmetric for the fast bulk data</li>
    <li>"Lossy" trap: encryption is not compression
        (<a href="../../lessons/9.3.html">9.3</a>) — nothing is
        sacrificed, everything is restored</li>
    <li>"A login system stores hashes of passwords" — why? So the
        service itself never possesses the passwords; a stolen
        database yields fingerprints, not secrets</li>
    <li>"What does a certificate prove?" — that the public key
        belongs to the named party, per a trusted authority</li>
</ul>

<h2>What can break it (and what cannot)</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Key in the lockbox with the treasure:</strong> your
        game encrypts saves with a key shipped inside the game. Every
        player IS the holder of the key — this is obfuscation, not
        security, and it is why client-side anti-cheat fails
        (<a href="../../lessons/9.8.html">9.8</a>'s leaderboard
        lesson: the client can lie; the server must verify)</li>
    <li><strong>Quantum:</strong> if factoring huge numbers becomes
        easy, asymmetric crypto breaks (symmetric mostly survives) —
        which is why standards are migrating to post-quantum
        algorithms now</li>
    <li><strong>Implementation bugs, not math bugs:</strong> the
        algorithms are fine; the code around them (random number
        generation, certificate checking) is where real attacks
        happen</li>
</ul>

<h2>Where you meet crypto this year</h2>
<ul>
    <li><a href="../../lessons/9.9.html">9.9 — Encryption</a> —
        the lesson (Caesar by hand, reading your own certificate)</li>
    <li><a href="the-internet.html">The Internet reference</a> —
        the packets TLS wraps</li>
    <li><a href="../unit-09/index.html">Unit 9</a> — the full
        data &amp; systems map</li>
</ul>

<div class="unit-nav">
    <span><a href="the-internet.html">← The Internet</a></span>
    <span><a href="../index.html">All docs</a></span>
    <span><a href="../unit-10/index.html">Unit 10: Jam Season →</a></span>
</div>`;
